WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
Most cloud deployments run through CI/CD, so pipeline design is part of deployment security.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:17.880
Branch filters, protected tags, release approvals, environment gates, runner permissions, secret scope, deployment windows, and manual approvals all influence what a script can do.

3
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.860
A deployment helper is only as safe as the path that is allowed to trigger it.

4
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:30.500
Validation, build, publish, deploy, and rollback jobs should not all share the same authority.

5
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.040
Validation may need little or no secret access.

6
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.500
Build jobs may need dependency and packaging access.

7
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.400
Publish jobs may need registry permissions.

8
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.700
Deployment jobs need environment-specific authority.

9
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.160
Rollback jobs may need a separate operational path.

10
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:53.100
Separating these permissions reduces blast radius and makes pipeline behavior easier to reason about.

11
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.860
Low-trust triggers should not receive production secrets or deployment rights.

12
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:07.320
A pull request, dependency update, or unreviewed branch should still be able to receive useful feedback, but it should not reach privileged production paths.

13
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:18.700
Protected branches, protected tags, release workflows, environment approvals, and scoped secrets help ensure that production deployment happens only after the appropriate review.

14
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:22.980
Promotion workflows should make artifact movement clear.

15
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:30.040
Operators should be able to tell which artifact was built, where it was tested, who approved it, and which controls applied before production.

16
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:38.540
Staged rollout, limited exposure, health checks, and measured promotion reduce blast radius when something unexpected occurs.

17
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.040
Good CI/CD gates are not paperwork; they are technical boundaries that keep deployment authority aligned with trust.
